      "text": "FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS — ask, don't assume\n• Who are the system users? (end users, admins, B2B clients, services)\n• What are the 3 core use cases to prioritize?\n• What is explicitly OUT of scope?\n• What are the read vs. write patterns? (CRUD-heavy, real-time, analytics)\n\nNON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS — the staff-level differentiator\n• Latency: P50/P99 read + write targets (not just \"fast\")\n• Throughput: peak RPS — ask about burst patterns vs. sustained load\n• Availability: 99.9% = 8.7h downtime/yr | 99.99% = 52min/yr | 99.999% = 5.3min/yr\n• Consistency: strong (linearizable) vs. read-your-writes vs. eventual\n• Scalability: current DAU + 10× growth horizon\n• Durability: RPO? (can we lose 1 hour of writes? 1 second?)\n• Compliance: GDPR (EU data residency), HIPAA (PHI audit), PCI-DSS\n• Geographic: single region, multi-region, global edge?\n• Security: public API vs. internal, auth requirements\n\nBACK-OF-ENVELOPE — anchor every component decision\n• RPS (avg) = DAU × requests/user/day / 86,400\n• RPS (peak) = avg × burst_factor (often 3–5×)\n• Storage/day = writes/day × avg_record_bytes\n• Storage/year = storage/day × 365 × replication_factor\n• Example: 100M DAU, 10 req/user/day → 11,574 avg RPS, ~35K peak RPS\n\nCAP vs. PACELC — frame before picking databases\n• CAP: when network partition occurs → CP (consistency) or AP (availability)?\n  CP: Zookeeper, HBase, etcd — blocks writes during partition\n  AP: Cassandra, DynamoDB, CouchDB — serves possibly stale data\n• PACELC: even without partition → optimize for Latency or Consistency?\n  PA/EL: DynamoDB (always prefer availability + low latency)\n  PC/EC: HBase (always prefer consistency)\n• Most web systems: AP during partition + eventual consistency at normal ops",
      "originalText": "FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS — ask, don't assume\n• Who are the system users? (end users, admins, B2B clients, services)\n• What are the 3 core use cases to prioritize?\n• What is explicitly OUT of scope?\n• What are the read vs. write patterns? (CRUD-heavy, real-time, analytics)\n\nNON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS — the staff-level differentiator\n• Latency: P50/P99 read + write targets (not just \"fast\")\n• Throughput: peak RPS — ask about burst patterns vs. sustained load\n• Availability: 99.9% = 8.7h downtime/yr | 99.99% = 52min/yr | 99.999% = 5.3min/yr\n• Consistency: strong (linearizable) vs. read-your-writes vs. eventual\n• Scalability: current DAU + 10× growth horizon\n• Durability: RPO? (can we lose 1 hour of writes? 1 second?)\n• Compliance: GDPR (EU data residency), HIPAA (PHI audit), PCI-DSS\n• Geographic: single region, multi-region, global edge?\n• Security: public API vs. internal, auth requirements\n\nBACK-OF-ENVELOPE — anchor every component decision\n• RPS (avg) = DAU × requests/user/day / 86,400\n• RPS (peak) = avg × burst_factor (often 3–5×)\n• Storage/day = writes/day × avg_record_bytes\n• Storage/year = storage/day × 365 × replication_factor\n• Example: 100M DAU, 10 req/user/day → 11,574 avg RPS, ~35K peak RPS\n\nCAP vs. PACELC — frame before picking databases\n• CAP: when network partition occurs → CP (consistency) or AP (availability)?\n  CP: Zookeeper, HBase, etcd — blocks writes during partition\n  AP: Cassandra, DynamoDB, CouchDB — serves possibly stale data\n• PACELC: even without partition → optimize for Latency or Consistency?\n  PA/EL: DynamoDB (always prefer availability + low latency)\n  PC/EC: HBase (always prefer consistency)\n• Most web systems: AP during partition + eventual consistency at normal ops",
