    },
    {
      "id": "g1B200Bdy",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 200,
      "y": 2460,
      "width": 1480,
      "height": 950,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "rEX9ZkQmLMonBL9XY3xl",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1015,
      "version": 2,
      "versionNonce": 1859569653,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782832720203,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "API DESIGN — select paradigm from requirements\n• REST: public APIs, CRUD, HTTP caching, universal client support\n  Use when: external-facing, resource-oriented, SEO matters\n• gRPC: binary protobuf, streaming, codegen stubs, low latency\n  Use when: internal microservices, high-throughput, strong contracts\n• GraphQL: client-driven queries, reduces over/under-fetching\n  Use when: mobile-first, multiple client types with varied data needs\n  Risk: N+1 problem → mitigate with DataLoader batching\n• WebSocket/SSE: persistent connection, real-time push\n  Use when: chat, live feeds, collaborative editing, notifications\n\nDATA MODEL — 3 decisions before schema\n1. Identify entities and relationships (ERD or informal)\n2. Map read access patterns (what queries will be most common?)\n3. Choose database TYPE from access pattern + consistency need:\n   • Simple lookups, sessions → Key-Value (Redis, DynamoDB)\n   • Relational, complex joins, ACID → SQL (PostgreSQL, Aurora)\n   • Flexible schema, nested docs → Document (MongoDB)\n   • Write-heavy, time-series, global → Column-family (Cassandra)\n   • Full-text search → Elasticsearch\n   • Graph traversal → Neo4j\n   Rule: pick DB from access pattern, NOT brand familiarity\n\nCANONICAL HLD COMPONENTS\nClient → CDN (static cache, geo-edge)\n  → Load Balancer (L7 ALB: HTTP routing; NLB: TCP gaming/streaming)\n  → API Gateway (auth, rate limiting, routing, SSL termination)\n  → Services (stateless; scale horizontally)\n  → Cache Layer (Redis: L2 distributed cache)\n  → Message Queue (Kafka/SQS: async decoupling)\n  → Primary DB + Read Replicas\n  → Object Storage (S3/GCS: blobs, pre-signed URLs)\n  → Observability (Prometheus + Grafana + OpenTelemetry/Jaeger)\n\nJUSTIFICATION TEMPLATE (use for every component):\n\"I'm adding [component] here because [NFR it addresses].\nI chose [technology A] over [technology B] because [reason].\nThe trade-off is [cost/complexity/consistency impact].\"",
      "originalText": "API DESIGN — select paradigm from requirements\n• REST: public APIs, CRUD, HTTP caching, universal client support\n  Use when: external-facing, resource-oriented, SEO matters\n• gRPC: binary protobuf, streaming, codegen stubs, low latency\n  Use when: internal microservices, high-throughput, strong contracts\n• GraphQL: client-driven queries, reduces over/under-fetching\n  Use when: mobile-first, multiple client types with varied data needs\n  Risk: N+1 problem → mitigate with DataLoader batching\n• WebSocket/SSE: persistent connection, real-time push\n  Use when: chat, live feeds, collaborative editing, notifications\n\nDATA MODEL — 3 decisions before schema\n1. Identify entities and relationships (ERD or informal)\n2. Map read access patterns (what queries will be most common?)\n3. Choose database TYPE from access pattern + consistency need:\n   • Simple lookups, sessions → Key-Value (Redis, DynamoDB)\n   • Relational, complex joins, ACID → SQL (PostgreSQL, Aurora)\n   • Flexible schema, nested docs → Document (MongoDB)\n   • Write-heavy, time-series, global → Column-family (Cassandra)\n   • Full-text search → Elasticsearch\n   • Graph traversal → Neo4j\n   Rule: pick DB from access pattern, NOT brand familiarity\n\nCANONICAL HLD COMPONENTS\nClient → CDN (static cache, geo-edge)\n  → Load Balancer (L7 ALB: HTTP routing; NLB: TCP gaming/streaming)\n  → API Gateway (auth, rate limiting, routing, SSL termination)\n  → Services (stateless; scale horizontally)\n  → Cache Layer (Redis: L2 distributed cache)\n  → Message Queue (Kafka/SQS: async decoupling)\n  → Primary DB + Read Replicas\n  → Object Storage (S3/GCS: blobs, pre-signed URLs)\n  → Observability (Prometheus + Grafana + OpenTelemetry/Jaeger)\n\nJUSTIFICATION TEMPLATE (use for every component):\n\"I'm adding [component] here because [NFR it addresses].\nI chose [technology A] over [technology B] because [reason].\nThe trade-off is [cost/complexity/consistency impact].\"",
