      "updated": 1782832720203,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "IDENTIFY BOTTLENECKS FIRST — then propose solutions\n• State the exact bottleneck: \"At 35K peak RPS, a single PostgreSQL primary\n  will saturate at ~10K writes/sec — that's the first thing I'd address.\"\n• Propose 2+ solutions per bottleneck with trade-offs\n• Pick one with justification tied to NFRs\n• Move to next bottleneck; don't go infinite depth on one\n\nCACHING:\n• Cache-Aside: app checks cache → miss → fetch DB → write cache\n  Best for: read-heavy, resilient to cache failure, cold start penalty\n• Write-Through: write cache + DB sync — fresh data, write latency cost\n• Write-Behind: write cache, async flush — low write latency, loss risk on crash\n• Thundering herd: probabilistic early expiry OR request coalescing (mutex on miss)\n• Hot key: shard key + random suffix (user:12345:rand(0,9))\n\nDATABASE SCALING:\n• Read replicas: async replication (monitor lag < 1s), routes ~80% of traffic\n• Connection pooling: PgBouncer (PG), ProxySQL (MySQL) — critical at scale\n• Sharding: Hash (even dist, no range queries), Range (ordered, hotspot risk)\n  Consistent hashing: virtual nodes minimize resharding impact\n• Indexes: composite covers WHERE + ORDER BY; partial for filtered subsets\n  EXPLAIN ANALYZE every query > 100ms; avoid N+1 with DataLoader or JOINs\n\nRATE LIMITING:\n• Token Bucket: fixed capacity N, refills at rate R — allows burst up to N\n  Best for: user-facing APIs, natural bursty traffic\n• Sliding Window Log: Redis sorted set + ZCARD — precise, memory O(requests)\n  Best for: strict compliance requirements\n• Fixed Window Counter: Redis INCR + EXPIRE — simple, edge-burst risk\n• Distributed: Redis Lua EVAL for atomic check+decrement; shard by user_id\n\nMESSAGE QUEUES & ASYNC:\n• Kafka: durable log, replay, partitioned ordering (partition key = entity_id)\n  Retention 7 days; consumer groups = horizontal scale up to partition count\n  Use for: event sourcing, audit trail, data pipelines, fan-out\n• SQS: managed, at-least-once; visibility timeout > processing time\n  DLQ after N retries; FIFO variant for strict ordering\n• Idempotency: consumer MUST store processed event_ids\n• Outbox pattern: write event + state in same DB tx → relay publishes\n\nCONSISTENCY MODELS (weakest → strongest):\n• Eventual: AP systems (Cassandra, DynamoDB default) — fastest\n• Monotonic Read: once you read version V, never read V-1\n• Read-Your-Writes: always see your own updates\n• Causal: cause precedes effect — collaborative documents\n• Strong/Linearizable: globally ordered — most expensive",
      "originalText": "IDENTIFY BOTTLENECKS FIRST — then propose solutions\n• State the exact bottleneck: \"At 35K peak RPS, a single PostgreSQL primary\n  will saturate at ~10K writes/sec — that's the first thing I'd address.\"\n• Propose 2+ solutions per bottleneck with trade-offs\n• Pick one with justification tied to NFRs\n• Move to next bottleneck; don't go infinite depth on one\n\nCACHING:\n• Cache-Aside: app checks cache → miss → fetch DB → write cache\n  Best for: read-heavy, resilient to cache failure, cold start penalty\n• Write-Through: write cache + DB sync — fresh data, write latency cost\n• Write-Behind: write cache, async flush — low write latency, loss risk on crash\n• Thundering herd: probabilistic early expiry OR request coalescing (mutex on miss)\n• Hot key: shard key + random suffix (user:12345:rand(0,9))\n\nDATABASE SCALING:\n• Read replicas: async replication (monitor lag < 1s), routes ~80% of traffic\n• Connection pooling: PgBouncer (PG), ProxySQL (MySQL) — critical at scale\n• Sharding: Hash (even dist, no range queries), Range (ordered, hotspot risk)\n  Consistent hashing: virtual nodes minimize resharding impact\n• Indexes: composite covers WHERE + ORDER BY; partial for filtered subsets\n  EXPLAIN ANALYZE every query > 100ms; avoid N+1 with DataLoader or JOINs\n\nRATE LIMITING:\n• Token Bucket: fixed capacity N, refills at rate R — allows burst up to N\n  Best for: user-facing APIs, natural bursty traffic\n• Sliding Window Log: Redis sorted set + ZCARD — precise, memory O(requests)\n  Best for: strict compliance requirements\n• Fixed Window Counter: Redis INCR + EXPIRE — simple, edge-burst risk\n• Distributed: Redis Lua EVAL for atomic check+decrement; shard by user_id\n\nMESSAGE QUEUES & ASYNC:\n• Kafka: durable log, replay, partitioned ordering (partition key = entity_id)\n  Retention 7 days; consumer groups = horizontal scale up to partition count\n  Use for: event sourcing, audit trail, data pipelines, fan-out\n• SQS: managed, at-least-once; visibility timeout > processing time\n  DLQ after N retries; FIFO variant for strict ordering\n• Idempotency: consumer MUST store processed event_ids\n• Outbox pattern: write event + state in same DB tx → relay publishes\n\nCONSISTENCY MODELS (weakest → strongest):\n• Eventual: AP systems (Cassandra, DynamoDB default) — fastest\n• Monotonic Read: once you read version V, never read V-1\n• Read-Your-Writes: always see your own updates\n• Causal: cause precedes effect — collaborative documents\n• Strong/Linearizable: globally ordered — most expensive",
