      "text": "SUMMARIZE — 3 key decisions + WHY\n• \"I chose [technology] because [NFR]. The trade-off is [X].\"\n• \"The most critical bottleneck was [Y], addressed by [Z].\"\n• \"If I were to improve this further, I'd explore [improvement]\"\n\nFAILURE SCENARIOS TO MENTION\n• Primary DB fails → automated failover (60–120s RDS), replica promoted\n• Cache cold start → thundering herd; mitigated by circuit breaker\n• Queue consumer lag → DLQ strategy, scale consumers\n• Network partition → AP system serves stale data; TTL-based convergence\n• Cascading failure → bulkhead, circuit breaker (open on N failures)\n\nMONITORING & OBSERVABILITY\n• RED (per service): Rate, Error rate, Duration (latency P50/P99)\n• USE (per infrastructure): Utilization, Saturation, Errors\n• SLI: measured value (e.g., 99.95% requests < 200ms)\n• SLO: internal target (e.g., 99.9% P99 < 200ms over 30 days)\n• SLA: customer-facing contract with financial penalties\n• Error budget: 1 - SLO = allowed failure budget (burn-rate alerts)\n• Distributed tracing: OpenTelemetry → Jaeger/Tempo (W3C TraceContext)\n• Structured logs: JSON + trace_id + correlation_id; never log PII raw\n\nSTAFF-LEVEL SIGNALS TO DROP IN\n• Cost: \"At this scale, the cache tier adds ~$800/month but eliminates\n  ~$8,000/month in DB over-provisioning\"\n• Org/Conway's Law: \"I'd align service boundaries to team boundaries\n  — Team A owns Service X end-to-end including on-call\"\n• Phase roadmap: \"Phase 1 handles 10K users (MVP); Phase 2 at 100K\n  adds read replicas + cache; Phase 3 at 1M evaluates sharding\"\n• Security by default: \"DB in private subnet, only accessible from\n  service tier; secrets in Secrets Manager, rotated weekly; mTLS internal\"\n• Operational excellence: \"Two runbooks I'd prioritize: primary DB\n  failover and cache stampede recovery\"",
      "originalText": "SUMMARIZE — 3 key decisions + WHY\n• \"I chose [technology] because [NFR]. The trade-off is [X].\"\n• \"The most critical bottleneck was [Y], addressed by [Z].\"\n• \"If I were to improve this further, I'd explore [improvement]\"\n\nFAILURE SCENARIOS TO MENTION\n• Primary DB fails → automated failover (60–120s RDS), replica promoted\n• Cache cold start → thundering herd; mitigated by circuit breaker\n• Queue consumer lag → DLQ strategy, scale consumers\n• Network partition → AP system serves stale data; TTL-based convergence\n• Cascading failure → bulkhead, circuit breaker (open on N failures)\n\nMONITORING & OBSERVABILITY\n• RED (per service): Rate, Error rate, Duration (latency P50/P99)\n• USE (per infrastructure): Utilization, Saturation, Errors\n• SLI: measured value (e.g., 99.95% requests < 200ms)\n• SLO: internal target (e.g., 99.9% P99 < 200ms over 30 days)\n• SLA: customer-facing contract with financial penalties\n• Error budget: 1 - SLO = allowed failure budget (burn-rate alerts)\n• Distributed tracing: OpenTelemetry → Jaeger/Tempo (W3C TraceContext)\n• Structured logs: JSON + trace_id + correlation_id; never log PII raw\n\nSTAFF-LEVEL SIGNALS TO DROP IN\n• Cost: \"At this scale, the cache tier adds ~$800/month but eliminates\n  ~$8,000/month in DB over-provisioning\"\n• Org/Conway's Law: \"I'd align service boundaries to team boundaries\n  — Team A owns Service X end-to-end including on-call\"\n• Phase roadmap: \"Phase 1 handles 10K users (MVP); Phase 2 at 100K\n  adds read replicas + cache; Phase 3 at 1M evaluates sharding\"\n• Security by default: \"DB in private subnet, only accessible from\n  service tier; secrets in Secrets Manager, rotated weekly; mTLS internal\"\n• Operational excellence: \"Two runbooks I'd prioritize: primary DB\n  failover and cache stampede recovery\"",
