      "text": "FIVE DIMENSIONS — interviewers score each 1–5 (25 max)\n\nCOMMUNICATION (1–5)\n1 = Jumps to solution, hard to follow\n3 = Explains each step, pauses to verify understanding\n5 = Teaches the interviewer; draws proactively; adjusts depth on signal\nTip: Narrate your thinking — \"I'm considering X because Y\"\n\nBREADTH (1–5)\n1 = Knows one technology per category\n3 = Names multiple options per category, knows rough trade-offs\n5 = Knows edge cases, failure modes, when \"popular\" choice is wrong\nTip: Always offer 2 solutions before committing to one\n\nDEPTH (1–5)\n1 = Can't go deeper than box-level diagram\n3 = Can explain implementation details of chosen components\n5 = Can whiteboard internal algorithms, data structures, failure modes\nTip: Know 2–3 components at implementation depth (consistent hashing, LSM-tree)\n\nTRADE-OFF ARTICULATION (1–5)\n1 = Picks tech \"because it scales\" or \"because we use it at work\"\n3 = Explains pros/cons of each option\n5 = Quantifies trade-offs (\"write-through adds ~2ms but eliminates stale reads\")\nTip: Always tie trade-off to a specific NFR from Step 1\n\nPROACTIVE PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION (1–5)\n1 = Only identifies bottlenecks when interviewer points them out\n3 = Finds obvious issues (single DB, no caching)\n5 = Identifies subtle issues (thundering herd, hot partitions, split-brain)\nTip: After each component ask: \"what happens when this fails?\"\n\nSTAFF-LEVEL DIFFERENTIATORS (beyond 25/25)\n• Mentions cost implications without being asked\n• References team/org ownership model for service boundaries\n• Proposes phased rollout with concrete milestones\n• Identifies security boundaries proactively\n• Shows awareness of build-vs-buy judgment\n• Discusses deprecation/migration path for replaced components",
      "originalText": "FIVE DIMENSIONS — interviewers score each 1–5 (25 max)\n\nCOMMUNICATION (1–5)\n1 = Jumps to solution, hard to follow\n3 = Explains each step, pauses to verify understanding\n5 = Teaches the interviewer; draws proactively; adjusts depth on signal\nTip: Narrate your thinking — \"I'm considering X because Y\"\n\nBREADTH (1–5)\n1 = Knows one technology per category\n3 = Names multiple options per category, knows rough trade-offs\n5 = Knows edge cases, failure modes, when \"popular\" choice is wrong\nTip: Always offer 2 solutions before committing to one\n\nDEPTH (1–5)\n1 = Can't go deeper than box-level diagram\n3 = Can explain implementation details of chosen components\n5 = Can whiteboard internal algorithms, data structures, failure modes\nTip: Know 2–3 components at implementation depth (consistent hashing, LSM-tree)\n\nTRADE-OFF ARTICULATION (1–5)\n1 = Picks tech \"because it scales\" or \"because we use it at work\"\n3 = Explains pros/cons of each option\n5 = Quantifies trade-offs (\"write-through adds ~2ms but eliminates stale reads\")\nTip: Always tie trade-off to a specific NFR from Step 1\n\nPROACTIVE PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION (1–5)\n1 = Only identifies bottlenecks when interviewer points them out\n3 = Finds obvious issues (single DB, no caching)\n5 = Identifies subtle issues (thundering herd, hot partitions, split-brain)\nTip: After each component ask: \"what happens when this fails?\"\n\nSTAFF-LEVEL DIFFERENTIATORS (beyond 25/25)\n• Mentions cost implications without being asked\n• References team/org ownership model for service boundaries\n• Proposes phased rollout with concrete milestones\n• Identifies security boundaries proactively\n• Shows awareness of build-vs-buy judgment\n• Discusses deprecation/migration path for replaced components",
