    {
      "id": "g3CommLftBody",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 594.2247549578747,
      "y": 7916.717408772298,
      "width": 1650,
      "height": 1425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "HnMfl3zUaLJez2eI6LWk",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 3052,
      "version": 288,
      "versionNonce": 1407457595,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782833663392,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "HOW TO STRUCTURE YOUR HLD PRESENTATION\n\nSTEP 1 — ANCHOR TO NFRs BEFORE DRAWING\n\"Before I start, let me make sure the design serves our NFRs.\nWe said [latency target], [RPS scale], [availability SLA].\nI'll call out which NFR each component addresses as I walk through.\"\n→ Do NOT draw until you've confirmed requirements\n\nSTEP 2 — WALK THE REQUEST FLOW (top-down)\nDon't list components; narrate the journey:\n\"A request starts at the client. First stop is the CDN — if cached\nthere, we never hit origin. On a cache miss, it goes to the load\nbalancer...\" → Shows you understand data flow, not just boxes\n\nSTEP 3 — JUSTIFY EVERY COMPONENT (template)\n\"I'm adding [component] here because [specific NFR].\nMy choice is [tech A] over [tech B] because [reason].\nTrade-off: [cost/complexity/consistency impact].\nGiven our [NFR], that trade-off is acceptable.\"\n→ Never add a component without a WHY\n\nSTEP 4 — IDENTIFY BOTTLENECKS PROACTIVELY\n\"The component I'm most worried about is [X] because\n[specific failure mode] at [scale].\nTwo approaches: [A] trades X for Y; [B] trades Y for Z.\nI'd go with [A] because [NFR linkage].\"\n→ Staff engineers surface problems before being asked\n\nSTEP 5 — INVITE COLLABORATION\n\"Does this design make sense so far?\nI could go deeper on [component] or move to [next area].\"\n→ Treat it as a technical conversation, not a monologue\n\nDIAGRAM DRAWING TECHNIQUE\n• Draw boxes first (shape), label second, arrows last\n• Label every arrow: what data, which protocol, sync/async\n• Start happy path; add failure paths after confirmation\n• Use dashed boundaries to group related components\n• Number components to reference them verbally\n\nHANDLING TRADE-OFF QUESTIONS\n\"Option A — [name]: [one sentence].\n  Pros: [X], [Y]. Cons: [Z].\nOption B — [name]: [one sentence].\n  Pros: [X]. Cons: [Y], [Z].\nGiven [our NFR], I'd go with A because [direct link].\"\n\nHANDLING AMBIGUITY (don't freeze)\n\"I'll assume [X] and call it out explicitly.\nIf that assumption is wrong, we can adjust the design.\"\n\nTIME MANAGEMENT\n• 0–5 min: requirements ONLY (no drawing)\n• 5–25 min: HLD (draw quickly, speak as you draw)\n• 25–40 min: deep dive (worst bottleneck first)\n• 40–45 min: wrap-up + Q&A\n• If running long: \"shall I continue with [X] or move to wrap-up?\"",
      "originalText": "HOW TO STRUCTURE YOUR HLD PRESENTATION\n\nSTEP 1 — ANCHOR TO NFRs BEFORE DRAWING\n\"Before I start, let me make sure the design serves our NFRs.\nWe said [latency target], [RPS scale], [availability SLA].\nI'll call out which NFR each component addresses as I walk through.\"\n→ Do NOT draw until you've confirmed requirements\n\nSTEP 2 — WALK THE REQUEST FLOW (top-down)\nDon't list components; narrate the journey:\n\"A request starts at the client. First stop is the CDN — if cached\nthere, we never hit origin. On a cache miss, it goes to the load\nbalancer...\" → Shows you understand data flow, not just boxes\n\nSTEP 3 — JUSTIFY EVERY COMPONENT (template)\n\"I'm adding [component] here because [specific NFR].\nMy choice is [tech A] over [tech B] because [reason].\nTrade-off: [cost/complexity/consistency impact].\nGiven our [NFR], that trade-off is acceptable.\"\n→ Never add a component without a WHY\n\nSTEP 4 — IDENTIFY BOTTLENECKS PROACTIVELY\n\"The component I'm most worried about is [X] because\n[specific failure mode] at [scale].\nTwo approaches: [A] trades X for Y; [B] trades Y for Z.\nI'd go with [A] because [NFR linkage].\"\n→ Staff engineers surface problems before being asked\n\nSTEP 5 — INVITE COLLABORATION\n\"Does this design make sense so far?\nI could go deeper on [component] or move to [next area].\"\n→ Treat it as a technical conversation, not a monologue\n\nDIAGRAM DRAWING TECHNIQUE\n• Draw boxes first (shape), label second, arrows last\n• Label every arrow: what data, which protocol, sync/async\n• Start happy path; add failure paths after confirmation\n• Use dashed boundaries to group related components\n• Number components to reference them verbally\n\nHANDLING TRADE-OFF QUESTIONS\n\"Option A — [name]: [one sentence].\n  Pros: [X], [Y]. Cons: [Z].\nOption B — [name]: [one sentence].\n  Pros: [X]. Cons: [Y], [Z].\nGiven [our NFR], I'd go with A because [direct link].\"\n\nHANDLING AMBIGUITY (don't freeze)\n\"I'll assume [X] and call it out explicitly.\nIf that assumption is wrong, we can adjust the design.\"\n\nTIME MANAGEMENT\n• 0–5 min: requirements ONLY (no drawing)\n• 5–25 min: HLD (draw quickly, speak as you draw)\n• 25–40 min: deep dive (worst bottleneck first)\n• 40–45 min: wrap-up + Q&A\n• If running long: \"shall I continue with [X] or move to wrap-up?\"",
