    },
    {
      "id": "g3CommRgtBody",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2304.2247549578747,
      "y": 7916.717408772298,
      "width": 1650,
      "height": 1475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "HnMfl3zUaLJez2eI6LWk",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 3054,
      "version": 398,
      "versionNonce": 217400955,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782833663392,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "8 SIGNALS THAT SEPARATE STAFF FROM SENIOR\n\nSIGNAL 1 — COST QUANTIFICATION\n\"At 1M DAU with 90% cache hit rate, ElastiCache costs ~$800/month\nvs ~$8,000/month hitting the DB directly. Cache pays for itself\nin week one.\" → Attach dollar amounts to architectural decisions\n\nSIGNAL 2 — CONWAY'S LAW AWARENESS\n\"I'd align service boundaries to team boundaries. Team A owns\nService X end-to-end: API, DB schema, deployment, on-call.\"\n→ Mention team topology without being prompted\n\nSIGNAL 3 — OPERATIONAL DESIGN\n\"Two failure modes I'd write runbooks for: [primary DB failover]\nand [cache stampede]. Alerts fire on SLO burn rate — not raw error\ncount — so we page on actual customer impact, not noise.\"\n→ Design for day-2 operations, not just happy path\n\nSIGNAL 4 — SECURITY WITHOUT BEING ASKED\n\"DBs in private subnets — no public route. Only the service tier\ncan reach them via SG rule. Secrets in Secrets Manager, rotated\nweekly. mTLS between services — no implicit internal trust.\"\n→ Mention security proactively as an architectural default\n\nSIGNAL 5 — PHASED ROADMAP\n\"Phase 1 (10K users): monolith + single DB.\nPhase 2 (100K): add read replicas + Redis cache.\nPhase 3 (1M): evaluate sharding or DynamoDB only after measuring\nactual access patterns.\" → Think in milestones, not final states\n\nSIGNAL 6 — DATA-DRIVEN THRESHOLDS\n\"Cassandra vs PostgreSQL depends on write:read ratio.\nIf writes exceed 30% of traffic, Cassandra's LSM-tree wins.\nBelow that, Postgres with read replicas covers us.\"\n→ Use specific thresholds, not vague \"it depends\"\n\nSIGNAL 7 — FAILURE FIRST THINKING\n\"What happens when the cache goes down? Thundering herd.\nMitigation: probabilistic early expiry + circuit breaker caps\nDB at 80% capacity. Remaining 20% get 503 + Retry-After\n— degraded but not down.\" → Walk failure modes unprompted\n\nSIGNAL 8 — BUILD VS BUY JUDGMENT\n\"I could build the rate limiter with Redis + Lua, but I'd start\nwith Kong's rate-limit plugin or AWS WAF. Rate limiting is not\na differentiating capability — buy first.\"\n→ Know when NOT to build custom solutions\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES TO AVOID\n✗ Drawing before clarifying requirements\n✗ Picking tech \"because it's popular / I've used it\"\n✗ Single point of failure with no mitigation\n✗ Consistency everywhere (unnecessary cost)\n✗ Not drawing anything (interviewers need visual anchor)\n✗ Asking too many questions before designing\n✗ Over-engineering for scale not in the NFRs\n✗ Ignoring security until the very end\n✗ No mention of monitoring or observability\n✗ Treating interview as a quiz, not a conversation",
      "originalText": "8 SIGNALS THAT SEPARATE STAFF FROM SENIOR\n\nSIGNAL 1 — COST QUANTIFICATION\n\"At 1M DAU with 90% cache hit rate, ElastiCache costs ~$800/month\nvs ~$8,000/month hitting the DB directly. Cache pays for itself\nin week one.\" → Attach dollar amounts to architectural decisions\n\nSIGNAL 2 — CONWAY'S LAW AWARENESS\n\"I'd align service boundaries to team boundaries. Team A owns\nService X end-to-end: API, DB schema, deployment, on-call.\"\n→ Mention team topology without being prompted\n\nSIGNAL 3 — OPERATIONAL DESIGN\n\"Two failure modes I'd write runbooks for: [primary DB failover]\nand [cache stampede]. Alerts fire on SLO burn rate — not raw error\ncount — so we page on actual customer impact, not noise.\"\n→ Design for day-2 operations, not just happy path\n\nSIGNAL 4 — SECURITY WITHOUT BEING ASKED\n\"DBs in private subnets — no public route. Only the service tier\ncan reach them via SG rule. Secrets in Secrets Manager, rotated\nweekly. mTLS between services — no implicit internal trust.\"\n→ Mention security proactively as an architectural default\n\nSIGNAL 5 — PHASED ROADMAP\n\"Phase 1 (10K users): monolith + single DB.\nPhase 2 (100K): add read replicas + Redis cache.\nPhase 3 (1M): evaluate sharding or DynamoDB only after measuring\nactual access patterns.\" → Think in milestones, not final states\n\nSIGNAL 6 — DATA-DRIVEN THRESHOLDS\n\"Cassandra vs PostgreSQL depends on write:read ratio.\nIf writes exceed 30% of traffic, Cassandra's LSM-tree wins.\nBelow that, Postgres with read replicas covers us.\"\n→ Use specific thresholds, not vague \"it depends\"\n\nSIGNAL 7 — FAILURE FIRST THINKING\n\"What happens when the cache goes down? Thundering herd.\nMitigation: probabilistic early expiry + circuit breaker caps\nDB at 80% capacity. Remaining 20% get 503 + Retry-After\n— degraded but not down.\" → Walk failure modes unprompted\n\nSIGNAL 8 — BUILD VS BUY JUDGMENT\n\"I could build the rate limiter with Redis + Lua, but I'd start\nwith Kong's rate-limit plugin or AWS WAF. Rate limiting is not\na differentiating capability — buy first.\"\n→ Know when NOT to build custom solutions\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES TO AVOID\n✗ Drawing before clarifying requirements\n✗ Picking tech \"because it's popular / I've used it\"\n✗ Single point of failure with no mitigation\n✗ Consistency everywhere (unnecessary cost)\n✗ Not drawing anything (interviewers need visual anchor)\n✗ Asking too many questions before designing\n✗ Over-engineering for scale not in the NFRs\n✗ Ignoring security until the very end\n✗ No mention of monitoring or observability\n✗ Treating interview as a quiz, not a conversation",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "b7H"
    },
    {
      "id": "g3TitleSecD01",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 621.4833964721436,
      "y": 9395.88435617164,
      "width": 2400,
      "height": 45,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
